    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "There's only one test class in the project, and it passes. The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec asked that owner responses return a `timezone` field derived from the region via the fixed table (NSW\u2192Australia/Sydney, VIC\u2192Australia/Melbourne, QLD\u2192Australia/Brisbane).\n\nChanges, kept structurally minimal (2 files, one function edited):\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `timezone` string property to the owner schema so the generated `OwnerDto` gains `getTimezone`/`setTimezone`.\n\n2. **`RespondWithOwner.java`** (a single-method class, so no surrounding class complexity to carry) \u2014 added a small static `REGION_TIMEZONE` map and one line, `dto.setTimezone(REGION_TIMEZONE.get(dto.getLocality()))`, right after the existing locality assignment. It reuses the already-computed locality, and an unknown region yields `null` (field absent), matching the \"absent for any other region\" behaviour.\n\nI left `RespondWithOwnerCreated` untouched \u2014 it deliberately omits locality/timezone, and the acceptance test asserts timezone on the GET response, which now returns `Australia/Sydney` for a Sydney/NSW owner.",
